\description{
Tabulates the omnibus test of the whole composition of a \code{\link[=comp_iv]{comp_iv()}} fit and
one "part vs remaining" effect per part. Each effect is the rotated model's
\code{ilr1} coefficient, or its slope at the reference composition (see Details),
back-transformed to the model's effect scale. Printing the result shows a
text report, as with \code{\link[=summary.lm]{summary.lm()}}, and \code{\link[=pub_ready]{pub_ready()}} builds a publication
\code{gt} table from it.
}
\details{
The effect scale is a hazard ratio for a Cox model, a cumulative
odds ratio for an ordinal model and a relative-risk ratio for a multinomial
model. A GLM's scale follows its link: an odds ratio (logit), a rate, risk
or mean ratio (log) or a mean difference (identity). Every linear engine
reports a mean difference.

For mixed models the per-part effects come from one refit per part (stored
at fit time in \code{object$models}), so building this summary does no extra
fitting. For an \code{"lmer"} fit, each per-part standard error, interval and
p-value uses the degrees-of-freedom method that \code{emmeans} uses for
\code{\link[=predict_realloc]{predict_realloc()}} on the same fit. The default is the Kenward-Roger
approximation through \pkg{pbkrtest}. The Satterthwaite approximation
through \pkg{lmerTest} is used when \code{emmeans::emm_options(lmer.df =)}
selects it, or when \pkg{pbkrtest} is absent, disabled or the fit has more
observations than \code{emmeans::emm_options(pbkrtest.limit =)}. Both
\pkg{pbkrtest} and \pkg{lmerTest} are optional, and when neither applies,
or \code{emmeans::emm_options(lmer.df = "asymptotic")} is set, the degrees of
freedom are asymptotic (normal). \code{"glmer"}, \code{"coxme"} and \code{"rlmer"} rows
are asymptotic, as their \code{emmeans} predictions are (\pkg{robustlmm} reports
no degrees of freedom).

When the formula gives the composition non-linear terms (see \code{\link[=ilr]{ilr()}}), a
product of coordinates or no main effect, a rotated fit has no single
\code{ilr1} coefficient to read. Each per-part effect is then the slope of its
linear predictor in \code{ilr1} at the reference composition, from
\code{\link[emmeans:emtrends]{emmeans::emtrends()}}, back-transformed in the same way, and the printed
report says so. With a composition-by-covariate interaction as well, that
slope is the equal-weight average over the covariate's levels. An engine
for which \code{emtrends()} gives no such slope (\code{"mblogit"}, \code{"lmrob"}) reports
\code{NA} rows.
}
